<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/lib/Code.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
import RangeTiles from './lib/RangeTiles.abide'
import rangeTilesSource from './lib/RangeTiles.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import totalsSource from './lib/totals.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import rangeTilesTestSource from './tests/RangeTiles.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import RevenueTiles from './lib/RevenueTiles.abide'
import revenueTilesSource from './lib/RevenueTiles.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import summarizeSource from './lib/summarize.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import revenueTilesTestSource from './tests/RevenueTiles.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="A count says which nodes an update touched">
        <Example
            id="count-a-range-switch"
            title="`measure(body)` — the DOM calls one click made"
            summary="The tiles total the last 7 or 30 days. Its test switches the range and asserts that three numbers changed and nothing was built."
            files={{
                'lib/RangeTiles.abide': rangeTilesSource,
                'lib/totals.ts': totalsSource,
                'tests/RangeTiles.test.ts': rangeTilesTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <RangeTiles />
        </Example>

        <p><code>measure</code> runs its body with the DOM's mutating members counted, and hands
        back a <code>Work</code>: one number per kind of write. The click rewrote three text nodes
        and two <code>aria-pressed</code> attributes. A version that rebuilt the tiles would show
        the same numbers on screen, and would fail on <code>nodesCreated</code>.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Row</th><th>Counts</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>nodesCreated</code></td><td><code>create*</code>, <code>cloneNode</code> and <code>importNode</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>elementsMoved</code></td><td>elements inserted or moved, with their subtrees</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>markersMoved</code> · <code>textMoved</code></td><td>comment and text nodes inserted or moved</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>dataWrites</code> · <code>redundantDataWrites</code></td><td>text written, and text written over the same text</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>attributesSet</code></td><td>attributes set, removed or toggled</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>classWrites</code> · <code>styleWrites</code></td><td><code>classList</code> changes, and inline style writes</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>listenersBound</code></td><td><code>addEventListener</code> calls</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>published</code></td><td>what a source on the page counted itself</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>An element moved and a comment moved are separate rows because they cost different
        amounts: only the element is laid out again. A DOM member the harness does not count throws
        naming itself while a case is open, rather than reading as zero, and
        <code>patchSet()</code> lists every member it does count.</p>

        <p>Under <code>bun test</code> the DOM is an emulator, and one preload registers it with the
        counting already installed. An abide app preloads the adapter instead, which adds
        <code>mount</code>.</p>

        <Code language="toml" caption="bunfig.toml" code={`[test]
preload = ["@abide/harness/preload"]`} />

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/ship/call-your-app-from-a-test')}>Tests</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Publish a count the DOM cannot see">
        <Example
            id="publish-a-summary-count"
            title="`publishedBy(work, source, fields)` — a count your own code keeps"
            summary="Three tiles read one revenue summary. The summary counts its own passes, and the test asserts one pass per range switch."
            files={{
                'lib/RevenueTiles.abide': revenueTilesSource,
                'lib/summarize.ts': summarizeSource,
                'tests/RevenueTiles.test.ts': revenueTilesTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <RevenueTiles />
        </Example>

        <p>A recomputation writes nothing to the DOM, so no patch can see it. The module counts it
        instead, on a record it puts under <code>globalThis.__HARNESS_WORK__</code> by a name of its
        own. <code>measure</code> zeroes every published record when a case opens and copies it
        into <code>work.published</code> when it closes.</p>

        <p><code>publishedBy</code> reads one source back by the fields you expect. A source that
        published nothing reads as <code>null</code>, never as zeroes, and a field the record does
        not carry throws naming it. A misspelled counter would otherwise read as one that never
        moved.</p>

        <p>The tiles read the same derived value, so the summary runs once however many tiles show
        it. Give each tile its own call and the test reports three. Only two tiles were rewritten,
        because the best day is the same over both ranges.</p>

        <p>The count costs one increment per pass, kept in production too. abide publishes its own
        the same way, as <code>wakes</code>, <code>bindingRuns</code> and
        <code>descents</code>.</p>
    </Section>
</Doc>
